        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/0010800002wkvaaAAA",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/0010800002wkvaaAAA/whale-and-dolphin-conservation-shetland",
            "name": "Whale and Dolphin Conservation (Shetland)",
            "description": "Whale and Dolphin Conservation works to protect cetaceans around the globe.  The charities main aims and objectives are to end captivity, stop whaling, to create healthy seas and to prevent deaths in nets.\r\n\r\nThe Shorewatch citizen science project is a network of volunteers trained and supported by WDC to carry out regular 10-minute surveys from Shorewatch sites across Scotland. We know that Scotland is home to a number of whale and dolphin species, yet there are still so many unanswered questions about where they breed, feed and travel. We believe it is important to keep eyes on the sea so we can better understand these amazing creatures to better protect them.  Understanding their movements will allow us to comprehend how our behaviours affect whales and dolphins. We can use the data we gather to influence policy makers and advise developers to ensure better protection for whales and dolphins. Shorewatch relies on our amazing community volunteers to collect this vital data.\r\n\r\nObjectives of Shorewatch\r\n•\tBring marine conservation expertise to local communities through attending local events, informing campaigns and supporting volunteers.\r\n•\tEngage local people in the process of protecting and conserving the marine environment and the wellbeing of local whales and dolphins.\r\n•\tInform conservationally minded marine planning by identifying specific seasons or locations of importance to suggest when development will have the least impact.\r\n•\tContribute to the designation of MPAs by providing data to government as they search for key areas of importance.\r\n•\tSupport monitoring of MPAs by collecting a long term data set which will show changes in the presence and absence of whales at key sites over time.",
            "website": "https://www.whales.org",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC040231"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/AnimalWelfare",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Animal Welfare"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/EnvironmentRecycling",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Environment / Recycling"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g0800000Gvfl2AAB",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g0800000Gvfl2AAB/shorewatch-volunteer",
                    "publishStart": "2021-07-13",
                    "publishEnd": "2026-11-27",
                    "title": "Shorewatch Volunteer",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nThe Shorewatch citizen science project is a network of volunteers trained and supported by WDC to carry out regular 10-minute surveys from Shorewatch sites across Scotland. We know that Scotland is home to a number of whale and dolphin species, yet there are still so many unanswered questions about where they breed, feed and travel. We believe it is important to keep eyes on the sea so we can better understand these amazing creatures to better protect them.  \r\n\r\nTo become a Shorewatch volunteer, you will attend a full day training session where you will learn how to identify different species of cetacean, and how to carry out a 10-minute Shorewatch. You’ll then be fully supported by our Northern Isles Policy Officer in carrying out your first few watches from one of our selected sites – in Shetland these are Mousa Sound, Eshaness Lighthouse, Sumburgh Head Lighthouse, Lerwick (the Knab), Belmont (Unst),  South Nesting Bay, Scatness (overlooking Quendale Bay), Burravoe (Yell) and Sandness.  All of the equipment you need to carry out the 10-minute survey will be accessible at each site. \r\n\r\nUnderstanding the movements of whales, dolphins and porpoises will allow us to comprehend how our behaviours affect them. We can use the data we gather to influence policy makers and advise developers to ensure better protection for whales and dolphins. Shorewatch relies on our amazing community volunteers to collect this vital data.\n\n## Experience required\n\nNo prior experience or skills are necessary - full training is provided.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nShorewatch is flexible and can be carried out any time and day of the week.\n\n## Travel details\n\nIn Shetland these are Mousa Sound, Eshaness Lighthouse, Sumburgh Head Lighthouse, Lerwick (the Knab), Belmont (Unst),  South Nesting Bay, Scatness (overlooking Quendale Bay), Burravoe (Yell) and Sandness.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g0800000Gvfl2AAB#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Shorewatch Volunteer",
                        "description": "No prior experience or skills are necessary - full training is provided.",
                        "commitment": "Shorewatch is flexible and can be carried out any time and day of the week.",
                        "applyLink": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g0800000Gvfl2AAB/shorewatch-volunteer?#modal_register_a0g0800000Gvfl2AAB",
                        "allowsRemoteParticipation": false,
