        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/0011p00002Y6zUMAAZ",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/0011p00002Y6zUMAAZ/guide-dogs-for-the-blind",
            "name": "Guide Dogs for the Blind",
            "website": "https://opportunities.volunteerglasgow.org/profile/GuideDogsfortheBlind",
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000I2GpVYAV",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000I2GpVYAV/driver-for-people-with-sight-loss-and-their-guide-dog",
                    "publishStart": "2026-09-17",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-03-16",
                    "title": "Driver (for people with sight loss and their guide dog)",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nDriver (for people with sight loss and their guide dog)\n\n## Description\n\nAs a volunteer driver for Guide Dogs, you&rsquo;ll be helping people with sight loss to live the life they choose by providing safe and friendly transport that enables service users or fellow volunteers to engage in Guide Dogs activities. The main requests for this role would be to transport adults with sight loss to and from our residential partnership training venues, this could be without the dog when they start their programme, and with their new dog when they are on their way home. Training venues are in Kilmarnock and Dumfries, and guide dog owners could live anywhere around the West of Scotland.&nbsp;\n\nThere may be times when there are more requests than others, so this wouldn&#39;t necessarily be a weekly volunteer opportunity, more as and when the opportunities arise. There may be the occasional request to transport a dog itself - this can be discussed during the application process.&nbsp;\n\nOn a typical day volunteering in this role, you can expect to:\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Provide safe and friendly transport to our volunteers, our service users and potentially their dogs, in your own vehicle or Guide Dogs&rsquo; vehicle. (Guide Dogs vehicle is located at Hamilton office, ML3 6JT).&nbsp;\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Follow Guide Dogs&rsquo; guidelines on transporting dogs.\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Maintain professional boundaries with all passengers and adhere to safeguarding policies.\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Sighted guide service users in and out of vehicles and premises.\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Check equipment is in working order.\nHowever, you won&rsquo;t be expected to:\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Transport others for non-Guide Dogs purposes e.g. for personal journeys.\n&bull;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;Transport anyone under the age of 18.\n\nIf you are interested, please click the &lsquo;Apply&rsquo; button below (available when registered and logged in). You will then be contacted by the coordinator by email or phone call. If you have not heard back after 5 working days, the first step is to contact the organisation with the contact details provided. You could also check your junk/spam folder on your emails just in case the response is there. If that doesn&rsquo;t work out, then please feel free to contact Volunteer Glasgow on info@volunteerglasgow.org.\n\n## Experience required\n\nMust have a valid driving licence and insurance (minor driving convictions may not preclude you from volunteering).\nA confident and conscientious driver.\n\n## Travel details\n\nGuide Dogs car located in Hamilton. Destinations include Dumfries and Kilmarnock. Collection of people with sight loss may vary in location.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nWe will reimburse mileage for pre-agreed travel using your own vehicle.",
