        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/00124000008nbX5AAI",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/00124000008nbX5AAI/rowan-alba",
            "name": "Rowan Alba",
            "description": "Rowan Alba is a charity set up to work with people who are at risk of becoming homeless. We believe that all individuals have the right to be treated with dignity and respect, and offer support in a person centred way, with the individual actively involved in planning their care.\r\n\r\nWe work with older homeless men and women in our supported accommodation and individuals in the community who are having difficulty sustaining their current accommodation. Many of our service users would benefit from more social contact and just having someone to talk to could make a big difference to them.",
            "website": "https://rowanalba.org/",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC036775"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/HousingHomelessnessness",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Housing / Homelessnessness"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g1p00000MVWtXAAX",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g1p00000MVWtXAAX/community-befriender",
                    "publishStart": "2020-04-03",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-02-28",
                    "title": "Community Befriender",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nBy volunteering with Rowan Alba Community Alcohol Related Damage Service you can end someone's suffering who has become lonely, isolated and barely existing in their own community, as a result of current or past alcohol use.  All you need to do is help us open these doors.\n\n## Description\n\nCARDS (Community Alcohol Related Damage Support) work with individuals who have a problematic, long-term history of alcohol use in the local community who are currently receiving little or no support in terms of their mental, physical and emotional wellbeing. These people, for various reasons, have been living the way we experienced during the COVID-19 pandemic. Some of them for most of their lives. We offer the support of a relationship with one of our volunteer befrienders and this becomes the vehicle to positive mental health and social outcomes. In your role as a volunteer befriender, you will befriend a client by spending time together 1-2 hours a week, establishing a trusting relationship and enjoying various outings and day to day experiences.\n\n## Experience required\n\nTo understand or have compassion towards those individuals who have had a difficult relationship with alcohol now or in the past. Being able to listen without judgement and understand the benefit of human contact and connection.\n\n## Training details\n\nWe will conduct an informal interview in person where you will find out more about CARDS. This is a platform for us to highlight all the areas you need to know and an opportunity for you to ask questions. The induction training takes place in-person with other new volunteers and covers basic things like communication, boundaries and learning from your allocated key member of staff. After a thorough risk assessment, volunteers are introduced to the client through double visits with staff. Further to our comprehensive volunteer induction training we provide ongoing training, equipping you with an array of transferrable skills. Training includes Blue Light Training, Neurodiversity, Alcohol-Related Brain Damage, Boundaries, Interpersonal Communication and other topics as identified by volunteers. These sessions will be held in person at our office in Fountainbridge. You will always be able to contact a member of staff for support if required. We offer group support sessions with other volunteers and one-to -one support from the Volunteer Coordinator every 6 months, as well as community sessions for clients and volunteers including Community Art Sessions, CARDS Saturday Cafe, CARDS cafe at the Botanic Gardens, the Scottish National Portrait Gallery etc.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\n2 hours per week for at least 6-9 months.\nWe ask that you prepare to spend the first two months joining the team and doing the necessary admin including your application to join the PVG scheme.  Only after this would you be introduced to someone and start to form a befriending relationship.\n\n## Travel details\n\nWe are a city-wide project so we have opportunities across Edinburgh. We try to match volunteers with the person who lives within a close distance and accessible by bus. The meetings will be decided between you and the client and will take place either in the individual's home , a local cafe or a community setting within Edinburgh. Ideas on city days out are welcome to widen the experiences of the individual.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nAll expenses incurred through your volunteering including travel and refreshments during visits are reimbursed twice monthly.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g1p00000MVWtXAAX#role",
