                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g24000000eI8vAAE",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g24000000eI8vAAE/carers-call",
                    "publishStart": "2026-06-24",
                    "publishEnd": "2026-11-24",
                    "title": "Carers Call",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nCarers Link provides information and support to unpaid carers who live in East Dunbartonshire or care for\r\nsomeone who lives in East Dunbartonshire.\r\nIf you look after a family member or friend who cannot manage without your support, you are a carer. You\r\nmay be “doing what anyone would do”, but we can help you.\r\nOur Carers Call dedicated telephone service supports carers in East Dunbartonshire. Volunteers call\r\ncarers offering a chat which may be about their caring situation or about anything at all. This can reduce\r\nthe stress and isolation carers can experience.\r\nVolunteers are not there to solve problems but offer support and contact. Carers feedback reports they are\r\nrelieved not to feel so alone and have access to other services offered by Carers Link or other\r\norganisations. Calls are made fortnightly, monthly or 3 monthly, so each caller has a dedicated list of\r\ncarers to call.\n\n## Experience required\n\nIdeally a good listener, someone who likes chatting, with an understanding of carers needs. Empathy,\r\ndiscretion and a non- judgemental attitude are important when hearing people’s different and sensitive\r\nsituations. Experience of being a carer would be advantageous but not essential. It is necessary to have\r\ngood boundaries and an awareness of confidentiality. Being able to support someone through whatever\r\nthey are experiencing is the main skill, as problems may not be resolved.\n\n## Training details\n\nInduction usually happens ether 1:1 or in a group of 3 or 4 Volunteers.\r\nTraining can happen in a group setting here at our offices, and Individual training can also be provided for\r\nspecific tasks. Informal day to day support and supervision can happen during the working day or by\r\narrangement.\r\nSupport and Supervision sessions are important to support our volunteers in the tasks they do. Sessions\r\nare scheduled every 6 months, but Volunteers are encouraged to speak to The Volunteer Co-ordinator if\r\nthey have any issue at all regarding Volunteering. Other members of staff are always on hand should the\r\nVolunteer Coordinator be unavailable.\r\nVolunteers also have the use of a confidential employee telephone counselling service and are welcome to\r\nuse this if they should feel the need.\r\nWe aim to make sure our Volunteers are well supported as they play a key role in our organisation and\r\nwork as part of our team.\n\n## Travel details\n\nCarers Call takes place from our offices in Milngavie, our offices are less than 10 mins walk from the train station and main bus stop, car parking is available.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nPublic transport is reimbursed and mileage is reimbursed at a rate of 55p per mile",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g24000000eI8vAAE#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Carers Call",
                        "description": "Ideally a good listener, someone who likes chatting, with an understanding of carers needs. Empathy,\r\ndiscretion and a non- judgemental attitude are important when hearing people’s different and sensitive\r\nsituations. Experience of being a carer would be advantageous but not essential. It is necessary to have\r\ngood boundaries and an awareness of confidentiality. Being able to support someone through whatever\r\nthey are experiencing is the main skill, as problems may not be resolved.",
