        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/00124000008nYGuAAM",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/00124000008nYGuAAM/royal-deeside-railway-preservation-society",
            "name": "Royal Deeside Railway Preservation Society",
            "description": "To preserve as a working railway for the education and enjoyment of the public, part of the former Deeside Railway line.  To colledt and preserve examples of steam and other locomitives, carriages and wagons and restore buildings of historidcal interest. To enable and encourage any members of the public to participate irrespective of gender, race or disability.",
            "website": "https://deeside-railway.co.uk",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC027686"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/ArtsAndCulture",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Arts and Culture"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/EnvironmentRecycling",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Environment / Recycling"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/SportLeisureRecreation",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Sport / Leisure / Recreation"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000INmXpYAL",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000INmXpYAL/train-guard",
                    "publishStart": "2026-10-07",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-03-30",
                    "title": "Train Guard",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nA Train Guard at the Royal Deeside Railway plays a key role in both the safe operation of the train and the experience our visitors have on the day.\r\n\r\nAs a Guard, you are responsible for helping make sure the train is ready to depart safely, communicating with the Driver, keeping an eye on passengers throughout the journey and dealing with any issues that may arise. You are also one of the most visible members of the train crew, so the role involves welcoming visitors, answering questions and helping create a friendly, professional atmosphere on board.\r\n\r\nIt is a role with real responsibility, but also one that is varied and rewarding. One moment you may be carrying out safety checks or giving the Driver the signal to depart, and the next you may be helping a family, chatting to visitors about the railway or making sure everyone has enjoyed their journey.\r\n\r\nNo previous railway experience is required. Full training will be provided, and volunteers will learn the role gradually alongside experienced members of the railway before being assessed as competent to work independently.\r\n\r\nWe are looking for people who are reliable, safety-conscious, confident communicating with others and comfortable taking responsibility. Most of our public trains run on Sundays, so regular Sunday availability is important for anyone wishing to become part of the operational team.\r\n\r\nBecoming a Guard is a great way to get directly involved in running the railway, learn practical railway skills and become part of the team that makes each service happen. It can also provide a route into other operational roles for volunteers who want to develop further over time.\r\n\r\nIf you enjoy working with people, like the idea of having a hands-on role and want to be right at the centre of a working heritage railway, this could be a very rewarding opportunity.\n\n## Experience required\n\nNo previous railway experience is required. We are looking for people who are reliable, safety-conscious, willing to learn and able to work well as part of a team. Good communication, practical awareness and the ability to follow instructions are important. Fireman training is physical and hands-on, so a reasonable level of fitness is useful. Regular Sunday availability is especially important, as most of our public running takes place on Sundays.\n\n## Training details\n\nNew volunteers receive an introduction to the railway and the Guard’s role, including the relevant safety procedures and responsibilities involved in operating passenger services. Training is then carried out alongside experienced Guards, allowing volunteers to build confidence and practical experience over time. Progress is competency-based, with volunteers supported until they are ready to be assessed for independent duties. Ongoing guidance, refresher training and support from experienced operational staff are available as volunteers continue to develop in the role.\n\n## Travel details\n\nBased at Milton of Crathes, a short drive from Banchory and close to Crathes Castle, the railway has free parking on site. There is also a bus stop across the road for those who do not have access to a car.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000INmXpYAL#role",
