                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g24000008kaNsAAI",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g24000008kaNsAAI/intandem-mentor",
                    "publishStart": "2017-03-22",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-02-28",
                    "title": "Intandem Mentor",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nIntandem, Scotland's mentoring programme for young people, brings together vulnerable young people aged 8-14 with a mentor to build a consistent and trusting relationship, and improve their chances in life.\nWe work with young people with poor mental health and wellbeing, those not engaging in education and lacking a positive role model.\n\n## Description\n\nYMCA Edinburgh provides mentoring support for young people aged 8–14 through three dedicated programmes: PlusOne, Intandem, and Intandem Kinship. Intandem and Intandem Kinship support young people that have ever been involved in the care system. \nAs a mentor, you will be matched with a young person based on shared interests and location. You will meet with that young person for an hour or two a week with the primary aim of having fun whilst also being a positive role model and supporting their access to hobbies and interests. The relationship is built on trust, empathy, and shared activities – helping young people feel heard, valued, and better equipped to navigate life’s challenges. For this long-term volunteering opportunity, the ability to be consistent and non-judgemental is key.\n\n## Experience required\n\nNo formal qualifications are required but you will need the ability to show empathy, be non-judgemental. You will be able to reflect on your own life experiences and learning as well as demonstrate the motivation to see children and young people succeed in life.\n\n## Training details\n\nInitial training will take place over two evenings, with optional additional training available. Training will cover areas such as child protection, safeguarding and mentor boundaries. (Paragraph 2)You will be allocated a coordinator who will attend the first meetings and provide ongoing support. Mentors will also have the opportunity to attend regular peer support, additional training events for personal development, and socials.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\n2 to 3 hours per week (including your travel time) with a minimum commitment of 1 year\nMentoring is generally provided during evenings & weekends during school term time (at times agreed between the mentor and young person) and ideally continues throughout school holidays.\n\n## Travel details\n\nAlthough our office is based in Leith, the majority of mentoring sessions will be conducted across Edinburgh at locations convenient to you and the person you are mentoring. \n\n## Travel expenses\n\nTravel expenses are reimbursed, as well as any money spent on activities with the young person, like bubble tea or tickets to a gig. Expenses can be claimed weekly via a simple online form.",
