            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000HptofYAB",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000HptofYAB/support-line-volunteer-children-first",
                    "publishStart": "2026-07-31",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-01-17",
                    "title": "Support Line Volunteer - Children First",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nCould you be the person who helps a family take the first step towards a brighter future? When families need someone to listen, our Support Line volunteers are there. By offering compassionate support and guidance you'll help families navigate challenges, strengthen relationships and ensure children have the safe and happy childhood they deserve.\r\n\r\nChildren First is Scotland’s national children’s charity. We stand up for children, keep them safe and support them to recover from trauma and abuse through our national and local services. \r\n\r\nOur Support Line provides compassionate advice and emotional and practical support to families across Scotland and anyone concerned about a child's safety or wellbeing. By making it easier to access help, we can help transform lives and reduce child poverty. Volunteering with the Support Line is a chance to make a real difference to children and families across Scotland.\r\n\r\n“I would say if you’re thinking about volunteering, just go for it. Everyone has something to bring, and it can lead you to places that you might not have thought about or even thought possible.” (Support Line Volunteer)\r\n\r\nWhat will I be doing?\r\n\r\nAs a Support Line Volunteer, you'll provide emotional and practical support to adults by phone, webchat and email. You'll help people explore concerns about parenting, family life and the safety and wellbeing of children, supporting them to identify what might work best for their family. Working alongside staff you'll help ensure children's safety and wellbeing remain at the centre of every contact. \r\n\r\n“No matter how many calls I have taken, I still find that there are no such things as two identical calls. Every person is an individual with their own particular issues and this keeps you interested in the role.” (Support Line Volunteer)\r\n\r\nYou'll become part of a friendly and supportive team, with trained staff always on hand to provide guidance and support. \r\n\r\nWhat will I get from volunteering?\r\n\r\nVolunteering with us is more than just giving us your time, we want you to gain something from it as well. Volunteering is a great way to develop valuable communication, problem-solving and listening skills while making a meaningful difference to families across Scotland. You'll gain experience of supporting people through challenging situations, work as part of a skilled team and build confidence that can benefit both your personal and professional life.\r\n\r\n“Training to be a Support Line Volunteer and all the subsequent time volunteering has really helped me build some skills which I wish I'd built years ago. Many of these I'd call 'life skills' which I can usefully apply in many different parts of my life.” (Support Line Volunteer)\n\n## Experience required\n\nWe're looking for caring, reliable people with good listening and communication skills who can respond with empathy and without judgement and support others through difficult moments. No specific previous experience or formal qualifications are required but volunteers must be aged 18 or over. If you can give your time, bring empathy and want to make a real difference for children and families, we'll provide the training and support you need to succeed.\r\n\r\nWe welcome applications from people from all backgrounds and walks of life. We know that families benefit from hearing a range of experiences and perspectives. Men remain under-represented within our volunteer team and we'd welcome applications from men who are passionate about supporting children and families.\n\n## Training details\n\nWe provide comprehensive training to equip you with the knowledge, skills and confidence needed for the role. \r\n\r\n‘I found the training incredibly comprehensive, and I came out of it with the confidence to begin taking calls and with the knowledge that I would receive a lot of support whenever I needed it’ (Support Line Volunteer)\r\n\r\nOur next training course will run on the dates below from our office in Bruntsfield, Edinburgh (please note that we need you to be available for all of the dates):\r\n•\tSaturday 24 October, 9.30am to 4.30pm\r\n•\tSaturday 31 October, 9.30am to 4.30pm\r\n•\tSaturday 7 November, 9.30am to 4.30pm\r\n•\tSaturday 14 November, 9.30am to 4.30pm\r\n•\tSaturday 21 November, 9.30am to 4.30pm\r\n•\tThursday 28 January, 5.45pm to 9.00pm (takes place after starting to volunteer)\r\n \t\r\nYou will be supported by staff when volunteering and you'll have a named supervisor who will help you develop your skills, build confidence and make the most of your volunteering experience.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nAfter training, we ask for a commitment of around 3.5 hours per week for at least one year. A range of volunteering shifts throughout the day, evenings and weekends are available from our Edinburgh office.\n\n## Travel details\n\nVolunteering is based at the Children First office in Bruntsfield, Edinburgh which is easily accessible on public transport\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nWe believe that volunteering shouldn’t cost you anything and you will be able to claim for reasonable out-of-pocket expenses for travel to and from our office in Bruntsfield, Edinburgh.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000HptofYAB#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Support Line Volunteer - Children First",
                        "description": "We're looking for caring, reliable people with good listening and communication skills who can respond with empathy and without judgement and support others through difficult moments. No specific previous experience or formal qualifications are required but volunteers must be aged 18 or over. If you can give your time, bring empathy and want to make a real difference for children and families, we'll provide the training and support you need to succeed.\r\n\r\nWe welcome applications from people from all backgrounds and walks of life. We know that families benefit from hearing a range of experiences and perspectives. Men remain under-represented within our volunteer team and we'd welcome applications from men who are passionate about supporting children and families.",
                        "commitment": "After training, we ask for a commitment of around 3.5 hours per week for at least one year. A range of volunteering shifts throughout the day, evenings and weekends are available from our Edinburgh office.",
                        "applyLink": "https://www.childrenfirst.org.uk/get-involved/volunteer/children-first-support-line-volunteer-edinburgh/?_gl=1*18lqui5*_up*MQ..*_ga*NjQxNzY4NDMzLjE3ODU0ODk1MDU.*_ga_B5ZQYQG926*czE3ODU0ODk1MDUkbzEkZzEkdDE3ODU0ODk1MTEkajU0JGwwJGgxNzc2MTQ0MTc4",
