                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000HoGvpYAF",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000HoGvpYAF/walk-leader",
                    "publishStart": "2026-07-24",
                    "publishEnd": "2026-12-31",
                    "title": "Walk Leader",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nOur Walk Well programme is an ideal way to keep physically active as well as being fun, free, and\r\nsociable. We have 19 Health walks already established across the area which last between 30 minutes to\r\nan hour and regularly we attract 10 plus participants a week.\r\nDescribe the volunteering Opportunity\r\nEDLC supports a network of Health Walks across East Dunbartonshire, supported by Paths for All and the\r\nNHS. Our walks are free of charge and offer a range of benefits including improved fitness, mood, and\r\nsocial interaction.\r\nI am looking to recruit walk leaders who will lead the Health Walks. As a walk leader you can enable\r\npeople to walk together in a safe, friendly, and supportive way, which in turn helps maintain the health and\r\nsocial wellbeing of your local community.\r\nWe invite potential volunteers who aren't already involved, to join us for a few walks (as a walker) before\r\ncommitting to volunteering to give you a chance to experience a Health Walk in action and to get more of a\r\nfeel for the role and its responsibilities.\r\nResponsibilities\r\nPlanning and leading regular walks, often jointly with other walk leaders. You will be welcoming walkers\r\nand helping to create a friendly and inclusive atmosphere.\r\nBriefing walkers about the route and any identified risks.\r\nSupporting new and less experienced participants to build their confidence and motivation.\r\nCarrying out site visits to check out routes, completing risk assessments of new routes and reporting any\r\nserious incidents. Skills development for the volunteers include leadership, management, and\r\ncommunication skills. They have the chance to meet new people, socialise with other volunteers and be\r\npart of a team.\r\nThese starting points are always accessible and convenient to travel to and are normally at a public place, community\r\ncentre or leisure centre. After the walk we can offer tea and coffee for walk leaders and walkers.\n\n## Training details\n\nInitial induction to the Walk Leader role and intro to other volunteers and to your processes for running the\r\nwalks, sharing walker info.\r\nThe volunteer will complete a walk leader training course which will be on zoom for 3 ½ hours and they can\r\ncomplete optional training such as Dementia Friendly and 1st Aid training for personal development.\r\nI hold quarterly team meetings with my volunteers, and we have a closed Facebook group we use to\r\ncommunicate in between, and I send a fortnightly email with updates.\r\nI will hold socials for the walking groups once every two months and we have a Christmas dinner to\r\ncelebrate the walkers and walk leaders’ achievements.\n\n## Travel details\n\nVarious areas surrounding East Dunbartonshire such as Kirkintilloch, Milngavie, Bearsden, Lenzie and\r\nBishopbriggs. I’m going to be setting up walks at key community spots to start the Health Walks. The\r\nvenue for these Kirkintilloch Leisure Centre, Leisuredrome, Lenzie Train station, Kilmardinny House and\r\nMilngavie Doocot. You are expected to make your own way there.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nYes",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
