                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD000008skZ7YAI",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD000008skZ7YAI/support-group-facilitator",
                    "publishStart": "2025-03-11",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-02-01",
                    "title": "Support Group Facilitator",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nAs a support group facilitator you would be facilitating a Bipolar peer support group, encouraging peer support and peer led discussion amongst group members in a welcoming, safe and non-judgmental setting.\n\n## Description\n\nWe run a number of online and in-person support groups across Scotland that bring together people living with bipolar to share and learn from one another. To do this, we need passionate facilitators to help ensure the groups take place regularly and are welcoming, safe, inclusive and positive for everyone who attends. \r\n\r\nAlongside a co-facilitator, you will facilitate a support group once per month. You’ll be welcoming people, encouraging discussion and dealing sensitively with issues or conflict that may arise within the group meeting. You’ll play an important role in ensuring meetings run to time and that everyone has an opportunity to share if they choose to do so. You’ll be responsible for emailing meeting reminders to group participants and submitting brief statistics to Bipolar Scotland after each meeting.\n\n## Experience required\n\nWe welcome applications from people living with bipolar, or experience supporting others with bipolar or another mental health condition.\r\n\r\nYou’ll have a passion for supporting and empowering others, excellent listening and communication skills and good IT skills. \r\n\r\nIn addition, you’ll have a commitment to confidentiality.\r\n\r\nExperience of engaging with groups previously would be an advantage, but not necessary. Having belief that everyone can work towards their own personal mental health recovery, and an understanding that this will look different for each person.\r\n\r\nThe ability to commit to facilitating a meeting once per month– reliability is essential.\n\n## Training details\n\nVolunteers will participate in 3 facilitator training sessions and 1 adult safeguarding session.\n\n## Travel details\n\nDundee Volunteer and Voluntary Action (Camperdown Room), 10 Constitution Rd, Dundee DD1 1L\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nTravel expenses can be reimbursed on receipt of expense form.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD000008skZ7YAI#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Support Group Facilitator",
                        "description": "We welcome applications from people living with bipolar, or experience supporting others with bipolar or another mental health condition.\r\n\r\nYou’ll have a passion for supporting and empowering others, excellent listening and communication skills and good IT skills. \r\n\r\nIn addition, you’ll have a commitment to confidentiality.\r\n\r\nExperience of engaging with groups previously would be an advantage, but not necessary. Having belief that everyone can work towards their own personal mental health recovery, and an understanding that this will look different for each person.\r\n\r\nThe ability to commit to facilitating a meeting once per month– reliability is essential.",
