        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/00124000009bMKIAA2",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/00124000009bMKIAA2/aberdeenshire-north-foodbank",
            "name": "Aberdeenshire North Foodbank",
            "description": "To mitigate the effects of poverty by the provision of food parcels to clients referred by a range of agencies",
            "website": "https://www.aberdeenshirenorth.foodbank.org.uk",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC045884"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/FoodBanks",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Food Banks"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000IU5BBYA1",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000IU5BBYA1/trustee",
                    "publishStart": "2026-10-13",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-04-13",
                    "title": "Trustee",
                    "description": "## Description\n\nWe are looking for three additional trustees to join an existing team of 5 trustees.  We are particularly looking for people who have experience of being a charity trustee, have worked in the third sector, or have a background in finance.  We run 5 food banks, which are spread over a large geographical area in North Aberdeenshire.  We also currently fund a financial inclusion service, which is provided by a partner association.  In addition to providing emergency food for people who are in crisis, we provide signposting to other organisation who can provide support, using the Aberdeenshire \"Worrying about Money?\" leaflet.  We aim to provide a warm welcome to clients, with refreshments if circumstances allow (although this is dependent on having appropriate premises, and at times can be difficult).  We have a large and committed group of over 100 volunteers, and currently employ three members of staff.  Training and support is provided to new trustees.  The Board usually meets every 4-6 weeks.  Approximately 4 hours per month are required for preparation prior to our Board meetings, plus additional time in between meetings for checking and responding to emails.  Additional time is required if trustees take on additional roles, e.g. in relation to finances or safeguarding.\n\n## Experience required\n\nExperience of being a trustee, working in the third sector or a background in finance is desirable but not essential.  A willingness to work as part of a team is essential.\n\n## Training details\n\nInduction training is provided by the Board of trustees.\nOngoing training is provided by Trussell and Trust Advice (an organisation which provides training and advice to charities).\nSupport for volunteers is provided by the volunteer coordinators, the staff team, and also by a counselling service, via Trussell.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nBoard meetings are usually held on a Monday evening, on the third Monday of the month, but this can vary depending on availability of trustees, and occasionally the meeting takes place on another evening, or in the afternoon.  Trustees can join Board meetings remotely, as all Board meetings are hybrid.  Trustees are expected to join all or most Board meetings, unless they are away on holiday, or have other pressing commitments (e.g. caring responsibilities).  According to the Constitution of the charity, if trustees miss three consecutive Board meetings, without good reason, they can be asked to leave.\n\n## Travel details\n\nWe currently run food banks in Inverurie, Ellon, Huntly, Peterhead and Fraserburgh.  Public transport to the food banks is limited.  Board meetings usually take place at one of the food banks, or in the home of a trustee.  Lifts can usually be provided, and Board meetings are hybrid, so trustees can join remotely.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nTravel expenses are paid.  If trustees require additional training, e.g. in relation to Safeguarding, this can be paid, if this has previously been agreed by the trustees.\n\n## Taster details\n\nPeople who are considering becoming a trustee can meet with one or two trustees in an informal setting, over a coffee.  They can attend a Board meeting as an observer, and can potentially visit a food bank (if this is arranged in advance and convenient to the person in charge of the food bank on the day, and under supervision from an existing trustee, although this usually happens during the induction period after someone has become a trustee).  In fact, I cannot recall a visit to a food bank of a potential trustee, and in general we would have some concerns about the confidentiality of the situation, and we would prefer to avoid this, and arrange a visit after references have been received, and after someone has become a trustee.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "image": "https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/scvo-volunteerscotland-search.appspot.com/o/opportunities%2F8mVxQK3abONdPF7NWkqGLyazcyB3%2FFm7eqrqljlFkylFy-thumbnail.jpg?alt=media&token=da7c3f62-895c-4a6f-abbf-3f10b0b4353c",
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000IU5BBYA1#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Trustee",
                        "description": "Experience of being a trustee, working in the third sector or a background in finance is desirable but not essential.  A willingness to work as part of a team is essential.",
                        "commitment": "Board meetings are usually held on a Monday evening, on the third Monday of the month, but this can vary depending on availability of trustees, and occasionally the meeting takes place on another evening, or in the afternoon.  Trustees can join Board meetings remotely, as all Board meetings are hybrid.  Trustees are expected to join all or most Board meetings, unless they are away on holiday, or have other pressing commitments (e.g. caring responsibilities).  According to the Constitution of the charity, if trustees miss three consecutive Board meetings, without good reason, they can be asked to leave.",
