        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/0012400000pm5SCAAY",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/0012400000pm5SCAAY/pet-and-companion-peace-scio",
            "name": "Pet and Companion (PEACE) (SCIO ),",
            "description": "The organisation's purposes are: 1. Primarily: the relief of those in need by reason of age, ill-health, disability, financial hardship or other disadvantages. 2. Secondary purpose: the advancement of health 3. Secondary purpose: the advancement of religion",
            "website": "http://www.petandcompanion.com",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC046851"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/FaithReligionBelief",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Faith / Religion / Belief"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/Health",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Health"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/OlderPeople",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Older People"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g0800000VuptPAAR",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g0800000VuptPAAR/pastoral-carer",
                    "publishStart": "2022-07-12",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-03-31",
                    "title": "Pastoral Carer",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nDo you have a heart for pastoral care; a lovely, gentle dog; and some time to spare?  At PEACE Team, you and your dog could provide canine therapy along with pastoral care to people in need of support and companionship, particularly: the elderly; palliative care patients; students; and vulnerable women.\n\n## Description\n\nPastoral Carers and their therapy dogs (PEACE Teams) visit people in need of support and companionship in Care Homes, on University and College campuses, and in community hubs.  PEACE Teams will volunteer for a couple of hours a week/fortnight in order to build relationships with the people they visit. The PEACE dog helps to provide a relaxing environment, while the Pastoral Carer offers both a listening ear and a means of support, happy to talk through any fears or concerns the person may have.\n\n## Experience required\n\nEssential: good listening skills, patient, caring, attentive, sensitive, discreet, trustworthy. Potential volunteers are required to have an active Christian faith.\nDesirable: experience in pastoral care.\n\n## Training details\n\nAll PEACE Pastoral Carers receive training in Mental Health First Aid, attentive listening and dementia awareness.  Volunteers will also become members of the PVG (Protecting Vulnerable Groups) scheme.  New PEACE Pastoral Carers will have an induction session with an existing Pastoral Carer.  Each volunteer is also advised of a main contact at PEACE or at the organisation they are visiting should they wish to discuss any concerns.\n\n## Travel details\n\nWe have a room at the Eric Liddell Community Hub, 15 Morningside Road, Edinburgh.  There are multiple bus routes that go past Holy Corner. It would also be possible for volunteers to visit Care Homes close to their own homes if more convenient.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nNo expenses.",
