                    "skos:prefLabel": "Sport / Leisure / Recreation"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/Volunteering",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Volunteering"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/YoungPeople",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Young People"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000BSwVlYAL",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000BSwVlYAL/adaptive-cycling-volunteer",
                    "publishStart": "2025-08-11",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-02-01",
                    "title": "Adaptive Cycling Volunteer",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nAre you a keen cyclist and do you want to help to change the lives of people with disabilities? Then Dundee Dragons Wheelchair Sports Club would love to hear from you.\n\n## Description\n\nDundee Dragons run adaptive cycling sessions at three locations in Dundee for children and adults. You don’t need to be a cycling coach, although that is useful. We are simply looking for people who are passionate about helping others. This could be as simple as setting out the adaptive bikes, accompanying the ride leaders or making teas and coffees for the riders. \r\n\r\nOf course if you wanted to get involved in being a ride leader at any level we can also make that happen. We can offer support to obtain sport specific qualifications as well as support to attend workshops on disability inclusion, first aid and child welfare and protection in sport.\n\n## Experience required\n\nEveryone has to start somewhere so a positive attitude, an open mind, an interest in sport and a willingness to “get in amongst it” is vital.\n\n## Training details\n\nThe induction training will involve initially attending sessions to meet the club officials and participants and being able to have any questions answered. Ongoing training will include workshops including Disability Inclusion, Child Welfare and Protection in Sport, First Aid if required, and any sports specific qualifications that the volunteer and club agrees would be appropriate.\n\n## Travel details\n\nDawson Park Dundee (Saturday 11am to 2pm), Dundee Cycle Hub (Tuesday 11am to 2pm), Castle Green (Thursday 11am to 1pm)\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nReimbursement of public transport costs\n\n## Taster details\n\nProspective volunteers, working alongside experienced coaches and club officials, can attend sessions to learn more about what the club does and understand how they might be able to fit into the programme. You don’t have to make any immediate commitment.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000BSwVlYAL#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Adaptive Cycling Volunteer",
                        "description": "Everyone has to start somewhere so a positive attitude, an open mind, an interest in sport and a willingness to “get in amongst it” is vital.",
                        "applyLink": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000BSwVlYAL/adaptive-cycling-volunteer?#modal_register_a0gQD00000BSwVlYAL",
                        "allowsRemoteParticipation": false,
