        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/0012400001KRXBzAAP",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/0012400001KRXBzAAP/food-train-scottish-borders",
            "name": "Food Train (Scottish Borders)",
            "description": "Food Train began in Dumfries in 1995 following a community survey of older people that found many of them struggling with their weekly grocery shopping; an idea was developed to ease this burden on older people.  A partnership of local shops and volunteers formed and Food Train began making deliveries of fresh groceries to older people in need with the help of local volunteers and shops.\r\n\r\nFood Train operated very successfully in Dumfries town area between 1995 and 2002 but the founder members knew there was potential to expand the service across the region.  A four year funding package from the Scottish Executive ‘Better Neighbourhood Services Fund' awarded in 2002 allowed staff to be recruited to develop Food Train and deliver grocery support across all Dumfries & Galloway.  By 2006 the grocery delivery service was fully operational across the region and the Board focused on the evidenced needs of the members and developed Food Train 'EXTRA' - an additional home support service.  More recently a third service has been added in Dumfries & Galloway, again responding to the needs of our members with a befriending service helping those experiencing isolation and loneliness.\r\n\r\nIn 2008 we began researching, planning and preparing to expand Food Train into other parts of Scotland.  With support from The Scottish Government, Community Food & Health (Scotland) and The Rank Foundation, we have been able to expand Food Train into West Lothian, Stirling, Dundee, Glasgow, Renfrewshire and North Ayrshire, providing food access solutions to many more older people and clearly demonstrating our ability to transfer our successful model geographically.\r\n\r\nFood Train is now a thriving multi award winning Charity supporting older people in 7 local authority areas in Scotland, with a view to developing further and wider across Scotland to bring food access to all Scotland's older folk.",
            "website": "https://www.thefoodtrain.co.uk",
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/OlderPeople",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Older People"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000HI13tYAD",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000HI13tYAD/delivery-driver-or-van-helper",
                    "publishStart": "2026-06-15",
                    "publishEnd": "2026-12-14",
                    "title": "Delivery Driver or Van Helper",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nVolunteer Delivery Drivers and Van Helpers support older people across the Scottish Borders by helping to \r\ndeliver groceries directly to members’ homes. Working as part of a friendly team, volunteers assist with \r\nloading and unloading shopping, delivering orders safely and efficiently, and providing a friendly face and \r\nregular contact that helps members maintain their independence and stay connected to their community.\n\n## Description\n\nVolunteer Delivery Drivers and Van Helpers play a vital role in supporting older people across the Scottish \r\nBorders to remain independent by delivering their weekly groceries directly to their homes. Working as part \r\nof a friendly team, volunteers help ensure shopping is delivered safely, accurately, and with care.\r\nThe role involves assisting with loading and unloading shopping at the store and at members’ homes, \r\nmaking sure all items are handled carefully and orders are organised correctly. Volunteers also help deliver \r\ngroceries to the door and provide additional practical support where needed, such as putting shopping \r\naway, unpacking boxes, and helping with simple tasks like opening jars.\r\nA key part of the role is providing friendly, respectful contact with members during deliveries, offering a \r\npositive social interaction alongside practical help. Volunteers support the safe transport and accurate \r\ndelivery of groceries, following Food Train procedures at all times. Through this support, volunteers help \r\nreduce isolation and ensure older people can continue living independently and comfortably in their own \r\nhomes\n\n## Experience required\n\nThe role is open to beginners but drivers must hold a full UK driving license and be willing to drive a van.\n\n## Training details\n\nVolunteers receive a full induction covering all aspects of the role, including safe handling of groceries, \r\ndelivery procedures, supporting members in their homes, and Food Train policies and expectations. This \r\nensures volunteers feel confident and understand how to carry out their duties safely and respectfully.\r\nNew volunteers are supported through a taster session alongside an experienced Volunteer Delivery Driver \r\nand Van Helper, allowing them to learn in a practical, hands-on environment. Ongoing support is provided \r\nby the Food Train team and fellow volunteers, who are always available to offer guidance, answer \r\nquestions, and provide encouragement throughout the volunteering journey.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nStaff are available for support during the working day and are on hand to answer any queries. Support can \r\nbe flexible based on need.\n\n## Travel details\n\nMeeting point is from 9.30am at Tesco Galashiels.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nTravel expenses are paid for bus fairs and fuel expenses. This is usually paid directly into your bank \r\naccount but cash can be provided on prior agreement.\n\n## Taster details\n\nNew volunteers are invited to attend a taster session where they are paired with an experienced Volunteer \r\nDelivery Driver and Van Helper. During the session, volunteers will get a first-hand look at how deliveries \r\nare carried out, including loading the van, handling groceries safely, and supporting deliveries at members’ \r\nhomes.\r\nVolunteers will observe and take part in key aspects of the role such as delivering shopping to the door, \r\noffering friendly interaction with members, and assisting with practical tasks like carrying bags and \r\nhelping to put items away where appropriate. The taster session provides a supportive, informal \r\nintroduction to the role and is a chance to ask questions, understand what the role involves, and decide \r\nwhether it is the right fit.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000HI13tYAD#role",
                        "type": "Role",
                        "title": "Delivery Driver or Van Helper",
                        "description": "The role is open to beginners but drivers must hold a full UK driving license and be willing to drive a van.",
                        "commitment": "Staff are available for support during the working day and are on hand to answer any queries. Support can \r\nbe flexible based on need.",
