        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/001QD00000mwrd3YAA",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/001QD00000mwrd3YAA/remap-lothian-fife-forth-valley",
            "name": "REMAP (Lothian, Fife & Forth Valley)",
            "description": "REMAP exists to improve the quality of life for people who experience short- or long-term disability through infirmity, illness, or ageing.\r\nWe do this by making and adapting equipment to meet the unique needs of individuals at no cost to them.",
            "website": "https://www.remap.org.uk",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC050584"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/AdviceInformation",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Advice / Information"
                },
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/PhysicalDisability",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Physical Disability"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000JmjskYAB",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000JmjskYAB/engineermaker",
                    "publishStart": "2026-11-11",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-05-12",
                    "title": "Engineer/Maker",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nRemap is a charity that design and make bespoke equipment for the Disabled, when a suitable commercial product is not available. Our aim is to improve the Dignity, Confidence and Quality of Life of the Disabled.\r\nAcross the Borders we help disabled people live the lives they choose. Our network of skilled volunteers respond to requests each year to design and make customised equipment free of charge.\r\nOur Engineer/Maker volunteers are at the very heart of what our branch does. They use their practical skills and creativity to help people with disabilities live fuller and more independent lives.\n\n## Description\n\nThis is a varied role where local need shapes what you’ll be working on, but it often includes:\r\nDesigning and making pieces of equipment for individuals with specific needs Using your practical skills working with metal, wood, plastic, or electronics (or a combination of them). Attending a bi-monthly meeting of the local branch.\r\nWe can support you to develop the skills you need for this role, and there’s an induction that covers all the areas above. You’ll also have chance to discuss your role and any additional needs with us.\n\n## Experience required\n\nEssential\r\nPractical experience in working with metal, wood, plastic, or electronics (or a combination of them) \r\nComfortable working with people, listening to them and understanding their needs\r\nAble to apply our risk management system\r\nAble to apply disability, equality, and inclusion practices\r\nAble to keep people’s personal details safe\r\nWilling to follow REMAP policies and procedures and a commitment to our values\r\n\r\nDesirable\r\nAccess to your own tools and workshop (or can access these)\r\nAble to drive or have good transport links\r\nYou’ll also need to complete the induction checklist, as well as a PVG check and reference check\n\n## Training details\n\nNew panel members usually do not have the full range of skills to meet every requirement. Across the whole panel, all the skills and abilities exist. Where necessary, members double up on a Job where the extra skill set is required.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nThe panel meet bi-monthly on Tuesday evenings.\r\nSupport for the client is delivered as and when required, and based on the availability of the Engineer/Maker.\n\n## Travel details\n\nPredominantly, work is completed at home, or at a local facility such as a Mens' Shed or equivalent. Due to the charity supporting the disabled, we normally meet with clients at their residence, typically with their medical adviser.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nAll expenses for travel and materials is re-imbursed.\n\n## Taster details\n\nNew panel members are inducted in Remap procedures and mentored via an experienced Engineer until confidence in the role is established.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000JmjskYAB#role",
                        "type": "Role",
