                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000I5NxhYAF",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000I5NxhYAF/volunteer-group-facilitator",
                    "publishStart": "2026-09-24",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-03-25",
                    "title": "Volunteer Group Facilitator",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nA fortnightly in-person peer support group for autistic girls aged 13-18 in The Borders, running 26 sessions throughout the year. Up to 15 girls attend each session, facilitated by 2 or 3 trained local women with lived experience of autism in women and girls, using our specialist neuro-affirming programme of topics in a low-arousal, low-demand venue.\n\n## Description\n\nA fortnightly in-person peer support group for autistic girls aged 13-18 in The Borders, running 26 sessions throughout the year. Up to 15 girls attend each session, facilitated by 2 or 3 trained local women with lived experience of autism in women and girls, using our specialist neuro-affirming programme of topics in a low-arousal, low-demand venue.\r\n\r\nPlaces are capped at 15 per session. Groups are age-banded, most commonly 9 to 12, 13 to 17 and 18 to 25, so that young people are with peers at a similar stage. Each session is led by two trained facilitators, both women with direct lived experience of autism in girls and women, whether as autistic women themselves, as parents of autistic daughters, or both. All hold enhanced DBS clearance and are trained in our neuro-affirming approach and in safeguarding, with oversight from our Designated Safeguarding Lead.\r\n\r\nSessions run in a low-arousal, low-demand venue. There is no pressure to speak or participate. Girls can bring headphones, sit apart, use the sensory and fidget resources provided or simply be in the room. Some attendees experience situational mutism and are never required to speak.\r\n\r\nSessions are interest-led rather than instructional. Facilitators work from a shared bank of activities and discussion prompts, chosen to suit the age band and adapted to what the group needs that day. Over the year, themes of self-understanding, self-advocacy, and positive autistic identity recur at a pace the group sets, rather than following a fixed syllabus.\r\n\r\nAutistic girls thrive with facilitators who feel safe, kind, and non judgemental. You need to understand that slow pacing is essential, building trust takes time and patience. Consistency builds safety so really reliable facilitators ensures predictable sessions. Respect for autonomy, our groups are co-produced so letting girls choose how they participate, communicate, and engage is very important to us. \r\n\r\nAsking supportive questions and showing curiosity, we want them to be able to chat openly, they don't always want advice just a listening ear -   “Tell me more about that” rather than “You should…”. You must be able to adapt activities based on energy levels, sensory needs, or unexpected overwhelm - these fluctuate from week to week and person to person. \r\n\r\nWe measure outcomes using the Good Childhood Index, with autism-friendly adaptations, at the start and end of each twelve-month cycle. Across our groups in 2025, 96% of attendees told us they felt happier, 94% felt more positive about being autistic, and 92% understood themselves better. Parents also tell us the effect reaches beyond the session itself, into sleep, school engagement, and family life.\r\n\r\nIf you are interested in finding out more about the role, please follow the link.  We will reply by email and we might chat on email a little before arranging an informal chat on Zoom or the phone. We usually contact people within 4 days of applying and then try to ensure we have a call arranged within 4 days after that.\n\n## Experience required\n\nLived experience is the most important thing we need. The best volunteers for our girls are those who were once autistic teenage girls and know exactly how that feels. Many of us joined AGN to provide the support, guidance and community that was lacking when we were teenagers. \r\n\r\nFacilitators require a neuro affirming understanding of autism in girls, trauma informed communication skills, and the ability to create predictable, sensory friendly environments. Experience with youth work, mental health support, or community facilitation is helpful but not essential. Personal qualities—warmth, patience, reliability, and respect for autonomy—are the strongest predictors of success.\n\n## Training details\n\nThis is to run a brand new group with funding we have received specifically to open a group in The Borders.  The location is still to be confirmed but it is likely to be in the Galashiels area.\r\n\r\nWe will support you to set up the group from the beginning and every step of the way. \r\n\r\nWe will provide training in Safeguarding, Health & Safety, AGN GDPR and Data Protection, Autistic Girls Network Values. We will ensure you are full trained on completing our registers, contacting parents, how to run your sessions and fundraise to keep your group going long term.\r\n\r\nDebrief with Lead Group Facilitator after sessions, Volunteer Coordinator on 'Slack' for a chat when needed. Chat each week you run a session especially for the few months or until you feel settled.\r\n\r\nCatch up date planned in for 6-8 weeks (after you've run 3 or 4 sessions) on Zoom.\r\n\r\nEach group has a channel on Slack so communicate with each other with the Volunteer Coordinator in there so you are never very far away from a friendly hello and an answer to a question\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\n•\tAs this is a new group, you can choose the day you run and we'll help you find a suitable venue and volunteers.\r\n\r\n•\tMost of our groups run on a week day evening but they run when it works for the volunteers and young people - usually 6-7:30pm.\r\n\r\n•\tYou'll be supported all the way by an autistic Volunteer Coordinator who also runs her own 2 groups.\r\n\r\n•\tWe will support you to set up the group from the beginning and every step of the way.\n\n## Travel details\n\nYou are responsible for your own travel.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nVenue hire and stipend for Lead Group Facilitator will be paid by us centrally. You will submit an expenses claim form to be reimbursed for any snacks, activity materials etc you need to buy each month. \r\n\r\nWe don't pay travel.",
