        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/001QD000019vw1iYAA",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/001QD000019vw1iYAA/alopecia-uk",
            "name": "Alopecia UK",
            "website": "https://opportunities.volunteerglasgow.org/profile/AlopeciaUK",
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000I2DrfYAF",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000I2DrfYAF/face-to-face-support-group-leader-to-run-alopecia-uk-peer-support-group-meetings",
                    "publishStart": "2026-09-17",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-03-16",
                    "title": "Face to Face Support Group Leader to run Alopecia UK peer support group meetings!",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nFace to Face Support Group Leader to run Alopecia UK peer support group meetings!\n\n## Description\n\nOur Volunteer Support Group Leads are the heart of our charity, offering vital local support to people affected by alopecia.\n\n&nbsp;\n\nAt Alopecia UK, we understand how powerful peer support can be. For many people affected by alopecia, having the opportunity to speak openly with others who truly understand can be incredibly reassuring and empowering. We know that alopecia can bring up a wide range of emotions, and being able to share those feelings, ask questions, and connect with others who simply &quot;get it&quot; can make a real difference.\n\nAlopecia UK has a network of over twenty-five adult peer support groups across the UK, offering regular meet-ups for those affected by alopecia. These groups are led by dedicated volunteers&mdash;usually individuals with lived experience of alopecia themselves&mdash;who play a vital role in creating safe, welcoming spaces for conversation and connection.\n\nIf you&#39;re thinking about starting a new support group, it&#39;s important to consider whether this role is the right fit for you. To help you decide, please take a moment to review the information below:&nbsp;\n\nWhat does an AUK Support Group Lead do?\n\nAs a Support Group Lead, you will take the lead in organising and delivering regular meetings, which includes finding and booking suitable venues, managing a dedicated AUK inbox, responding to enquiries, and sending updates to your group&rsquo;s distribution list. On meeting days, you will welcome attendees and help to facilitate open, inclusive conversations among the group. While Support Group Leads are not trained counsellors, they offer valuable peer support by sharing their own experiences, providing a listening ear, and signposting to further resources where needed.&nbsp;\n\nWhat You&rsquo;ll be Doing:\n\n\n\tOrganise, coordinate, and deliver local support group meetings\n\tFind and book suitable venues for meetings\n\tManage the Alopecia UK (AUK) support group inbox, responding to enquiries in a timely manner\n\tSend out communications to the support group&rsquo;s distribution list\n\tWelcome attendees at meetings and help facilitate conversations within the group\n\tComplete brief data collection forms following each meeting\n\tAttend a short online induction and training session with the Volunteer Manager\n\tParticipate in regular support group lead team meetings and one-to-one check ins with the Volunteer Manager&nbsp;\n\tAdhere to Alopecia UK policies and procedures at all times\n\tMaintain appropriate boundaries with support group attendees&nbsp;\n\n\nWhat makes a great support group lead:\n\n\n\tLived experience of alopecia, with the confidence and empathy to support others on their journey\n\tWarm, friendly, and approachable, with strong communication and active listening skills\n\tWillingness to openly share aspects of your own alopecia experience to help others feel less alone\n\tComfortable discussing sensitive and personal topics in a respectful and supportive manner\n\tReliable, compassionate, and non- judgemental, with a genuine desire to help others&nbsp;\n\tConfident using a computer and accessing the internet, with basic tech skills to manage emails and online communications&nbsp;\n\n\nIf you are interested, please click the &lsquo;Apply&rsquo; button below (available when registered and logged in). You will then be contacted by the coordinator by email or phone call. If you have not heard back after 5 working days, the first step is to contact the organisation with the contact details provided. You could also check your junk/spam folder on your emails just in case the response is there. If that doesn&rsquo;t work out, then please feel free to contact Volunteer Glasgow on info@volunteerglasgow.org.&nbsp;\n\n## Experience required\n\nWhat makes a great support group lead:\nLived experience of alopecia, with the confidence and empathy to support others on their journey\nWarm, friendly, and approachable, with strong communication and active listening skills\nWillingness to openly share aspects of your own alopecia experience to help others feel less alone\nComfortable discussing sensitive and personal topics in a respectful and supportive manner\nReliable, compassionate, and non- judgemental, with a genuine desire to help othe",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000I2DrfYAF#role",
