        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/001QD00001E2Yy8YAF",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/001QD00001E2Yy8YAF/epilepsy-connections",
            "name": "Epilepsy Connections",
            "website": "https://opportunities.volunteerglasgow.org/profile/EpilepsyConnections",
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0gQD00000Jk7mjYAB",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0gQD00000Jk7mjYAB/friends-connected-volunteer",
                    "publishStart": "2026-11-10",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-05-09",
                    "title": "Friends Connected Volunteer",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nFriends Connected Volunteer\n\n## Description\n\nHelp people connect, enjoy activities and feel part of a welcoming group.\n\nFriends Connected brings adults together to enjoy social activities, meet others and build friendships. As a volunteer with Epilepsy Connections, you&rsquo;ll help adults with a range of support needs, including people living with epilepsy, feel welcome and included.\n\nVolunteering alongside the Volunteer Coordinator and other volunteers, you&rsquo;ll help create enjoyable monthly activities where everyone can take part at their own pace.\n\nWhat will you do?\n\n\n\tHelp the Volunteer Coordinator plan and organise social activities.\n\tWelcome participants and help them feel comfortable, particularly if they are attending for the first time.\n\tOffer friendly encouragement and practical support during outings.\n\tHelp people join in conversations, get to know others and enjoy activities together.\n\tSupport group outings such as ten-pin bowling and museum visits.\n\tRespect each person&rsquo;s choices, interests and support needs.\n\n\nWho would this suit?\n\nThis role would suit someone who is friendly, patient and reliable, enjoys meeting people and works well as part of a team. A calm, compassionate approach and a willingness to adapt to different needs will help you make everyone feel included.\n\nTraining and support\n\nBefore starting your role, you&rsquo;ll complete a training programme covering epilepsy awareness, communication and managing difficult situations, alongside other topics relevant to your volunteering.\n\nYou&rsquo;ll receive ongoing support and supervision from the Volunteer &amp; Befriending Coordinator, with opportunities to ask questions, discuss concerns and seek guidance.\n\nTime commitment\n\nActivities take place monthly, with the time required depending on the outing. You may also help with planning and preparation.\n\nDiscuss the activity schedule, length of outings and your availability with the Volunteer Coordinator before committing.\n\nWhy volunteer?\n\nYou&rsquo;ll meet new people, develop your communication and teamwork skills, and gain experience of supporting inclusive group activities.\n\nYour contribution will help participants build confidence, reduce isolation and enjoy new experiences. You&rsquo;ll also help Epilepsy Connections offer welcoming social opportunities for people affected by epilepsy.\n\nInterested?\n\nClick Apply to express your interest in becoming a Friends Connected Volunteer.\n\nWhat happens after you apply?\n\nAfter you express an interest, you&rsquo;ll be asked to complete an application form, provide references and attend an interview with Heather Davies, our Volunteer &amp; Befriending Coordinator.\n\nIf you&rsquo;re successful, Heather will arrange the PVG application process and your training programme before you begin volunteering.\n\n## Experience required\n\n•\tFriendly and approachable: Help participants feel welcome and comfortable in the group.\n•\tCompassionate: Listen without judgement and respect each person’s experiences, choices and needs.\n•\tReliable: Attend agreed activities and communicate promptly if your availability changes.\n•\tPatient and flexible: Support people at their own pace and adapt when plans or needs change.\n•\tCalm and considerate: Respond thoughtfully when someone feels anxious or needs reassurance.\n•\tResilient and self-awa\n\n## Travel details\n\nFriends Connected activities take place at different community venues, depending on the outing. Confirm the meeting point and travel arrangements with the Volunteer Coordinator before each activity.\n\nEpilepsy Connections’ office is at Suites 129–134, Baltic Chambers, 50 Wellington Street, Glasgow, G2 6HJ, a short walk from Glasgow Central Station.\n\nReasonable out-of-pocket volunteering expenses are reimbursed. Check which travel costs are covered with the Volunteer Coordinator before travelling.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nReasonable expenses will be reimbursed on proof of receipts.",
