        {
            "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Organisation/00124000008ne2LAAQ",
            "type": "Organisation",
            "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/organisations/00124000008ne2LAAQ/link-east-fife-mental-health-befriending-project",
            "name": "LINK East Fife Mental Health Befriending Project",
            "description": "The aims of the group are to relieve the poverty, suffering and distress, advance the education of and promote the welfare of the inhabitants of East Fife area who have or have had  mental health problems and are identified as being socially and rurally isolated by the provision of a befriending service to encourage them to participate more fully in the life of the community.",
            "website": "https://www.linkbefriending.org.uk",
            "charityRegistration": [
                {
                    "registrar": "https://www.oscr.org.uk",
                    "registrationNumber": "SC027493"
                }
            ],
            "cause": [
                {
                    "@id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Taxonomy/Cause/MentalHealth",
                    "skos:prefLabel": "Mental Health"
                }
            ],
            "activity": [
                {
                    "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g24000000eODtAAM",
                    "type": "Activity",
                    "dc:source": "https://volunteer.scot/search/a0g24000000eODtAAM/befriender",
                    "publishStart": "2022-03-13",
                    "publishEnd": "2027-02-01",
                    "title": "Befriender",
                    "description": "## Summary\n\nHave fun volunteering; you could find yourself going to the cinema, playing sport, attending a class together or just having coffee and a chat. Benefits: on-going training and support, out of pocket expenses and group events.\n\n## Description\n\nIf you can spare a couple of hours per fortnight minimum and are able to volunteer for a year or more helping adults who have become isolated through mental health problems we would like to hear from you.\r\n\r\nYou will take your befriendee out to leisure and community activities and support them to do things they may be unable to do alone. Examples have included walks, squash, a knitting group, trips to a safari park, visiting museums, taking a class in IT skills, attending support groups, golfing, and just going for coffee and a chat.\r\n\r\nInitial and on-going training is given and other benefits include out of pocket expenses, regular support and supervision meetings, group events, Saltire or LINK Awards, reference after 6 months.\n\n## Experience required\n\nHonest, reliable, a good listener. Experience of mental health issues desirable but NOT essential.\n\n## Training details\n\nInduction training is usually held over 2 sessions which each lasts 3 hours. Support is at least monthly and in addition the project coordinator is available by text, telephone and email at other times.\n\n## Frequency commitment details\n\nMinimum 2 hours fortnightly.\n\n## Travel details\n\nWe will try to match you with someone close to you. We have people referred from us as close as Tayport and you can make use of having more facilities in Dundee by meeting there if your befriendee is able to.\n\n## Travel expenses\n\nExpenses of up to £80 per month paid for transport to and from befriending meetings and training, cost of any activities and light refreshments while out with the befriendee. Mileage is paid at £0.45 per mile.",
                    "requiresEmergencyParticipation": false,
                    "role": {
                        "id": "https://data.volunteer.scot/Activity/a0g24000000eODtAAM#role",
                        "type": "Role",
